WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.040
How does a pushdown automaton

2
00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:04.100
use its stack? It combines

3
00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:06.340
finite control with an unbounded

4
00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:08.900
last-in-first-out memory. We will trace

5
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:10.640
equal blocks of zeros and

6
00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:13.060
ones, decoding each read, pop

7
00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:14.980
and push operation instead of

8
00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:16.780
treating epsilon as a mysterious

9
00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:18.040
empty-stack test.

10
00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:20.460
A label has three separate

11
00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:23.100
slots: input read, stack symbol

12
00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.040
popped, and stack symbol pushed.

13
00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:27.260
Epsilon in a slot means

14
00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:29.060
no operation in that slot.

15
00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:31.280
In particular, an epsilon pop

16
00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:32.960
means do not inspect or

17
00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:34.820
remove a stack symbol, not

18
00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:35.900
that the stack is empty.

19
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:38.800
Initialize the equal-block machine by

20
00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:40.300
pushing a fresh dollar marker

21
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:42.840
without consuming input. The marker

22
00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:44.380
separates the bottom from all

23
00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:46.320
zeros we will count. This

24
00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:47.940
is a genuine stack operation

25
00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:49.620
even though the input cursor

26
00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:50.760
stays at the beginning.

27
00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:53.180
Read the first zero and

28
00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:54.420
push a zero above the

29
00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:56.600
marker. Read another zero and

30
00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.420
push another copy, so the

31
00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:00.420
stack records exactly how many

32
00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:03.260
initial zeros remain unmatched. These

33
00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:04.720
moves need no pop or

34
00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:05.980
prior top-symbol test.

35
00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:08.120
At the first one, switch

36
00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:09.360
from the pushing phase to

37
00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.420
the popping phase. Read that

38
00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:13.020
one and pop one zero,

39
00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:15.100
matching one symbol from each

40
00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:17.000
block. The stack now contains

41
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.760
one zero above the marker,

42
00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:20.800
with one final one still

43
00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:21.300
unread.

44
00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:23.620
Read the final one and

45
00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:26.040
pop the remaining zero. The

46
00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:27.620
marker is now exposed and

47
00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:29.480
no input remains, so the

48
00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:30.840
machine may pop the marker

49
00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:32.620
and enter its final state.

50
00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:34.660
If extra ones remained, there

51
00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:35.900
would be no zero left

52
00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:37.220
to match them, and that

53
00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:38.300
branch could not accept.

54
00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:40.880
For zero zero one, the

55
00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:42.400
input ends while a zero

56
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:44.480
remains above the marker, so

57
00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:46.100
the final marker-pop move is

58
00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:48.420
unavailable. For zero one zero

59
00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:50.400
one, a zero arrives after

60
00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.220
the popping phase began, where

61
00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:54.680
no zero-reading continuation is provided.

62
00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:57.060
Equal counts alone are insufficient;

63
00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:59.180
the required block order matters

64
00:01:59.180 --> 00:01:59.560
too.

65
00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:01.800
The case n equal to

66
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:04.600
zero requires accepting epsilon. The

67
00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:06.280
lecture’s machine makes its original

68
00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:08.440
start accepting for exactly this

69
00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:10.540
purpose, while its later final

70
00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:13.000
state handles nonempty matched blocks.

71
00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:14.500
Being at a final state

72
00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:17.020
never authorizes ignoring unread input.

73
00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:20.080
The general formal definition accepts

74
00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:22.140
by final state after complete

75
00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:24.300
input consumption. It does not

76
00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:25.980
demand an empty final stack

77
00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:28.020
for every possible pushdown automaton.

78
00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:31.240
Some constructions, including the grammar-equivalence

79
00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:34.380
normalization, deliberately arrange empty-stack acceptance

80
00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:35.880
as an additional invariant.

81
00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:37.500
Things to take note of.

82
00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:39.680
Decode the three transition slots

83
00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:42.640
independently, write every intermediate stack

84
00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:43.500
with its top on the

85
00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:45.160
left, and include the epsilon

86
00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:47.140
moves in your trace. Explain

87
00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:49.700
both the unmatched-count invariant and

88
00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:51.560
the phase restriction, then check

89
00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:53.880
epsilon, too few ones, too

90
00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:56.160
many ones and wrong-order inputs.
